1
00:00:00,180 --> 00:00:02,660
You know exactly what is wrong with your face.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:08,780
Nobody else has ever seen it. Not once, not in all the years of people looking straight at you.

3
00:00:08,860 --> 00:00:12,920
You call that politeness. Or your own vanity finally showing.

4
00:00:13,180 --> 00:00:16,359
But it is neither. The gap between what you see

5
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:20,140
and what they see has been measured, and it has a name:

6
00:00:20,530 --> 00:00:26,040
the spotlight effect. Ask people to guess how many notice the thing they are self-conscious

7
00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:28,480
about, and they say about half the room.

8
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:33,360
The real number is closer to a quarter. So where does the other half live,

9
00:00:33,820 --> 00:00:37,020
and why is your face the one place you cannot check?

10
00:00:37,530 --> 00:00:39,380
The measurement came from a T-shirt.

11
00:00:40,140 --> 00:00:43,240
In 2000, the psychologists Thomas Gilovich,

12
00:00:43,380 --> 00:00:49,240
Victoria Medvec and Kenneth Savitsky asked students to put on a shirt printed with an outdated

13
00:00:49,320 --> 00:00:52,690
singer's face, picked because it was mildly embarrassing,

14
00:00:52,890 --> 00:00:55,900
then walk into a room full of their peers.

15
00:00:55,980 --> 00:01:00,600
Before going in, each student guessed how many people would notice the shirt and remember it.

16
00:01:01,140 --> 00:01:03,800
Afterward, the researchers asked the room directly.

17
00:01:04,060 --> 00:01:09,020
The wearers guessed roughly half. Roughly a quarter had actually clocked it.

18
00:01:09,640 --> 00:01:12,640
Where did the other half go? Nowhere.

19
00:01:12,720 --> 00:01:17,260
It never existed outside one head. The wearer had been staring at that shirt from the second

20
00:01:17,340 --> 00:01:21,220
they pulled it on, and everyone else had glanced once and gone straight back to the thing they

21
00:01:21,300 --> 00:01:24,610
were already worrying about, which was almost certainly themselves.

22
00:01:25,380 --> 00:01:29,580
That is the whole mechanism, and it is oddly gentle once you see it.

23
00:01:29,660 --> 00:01:34,120
You are stuck at the exact centre of your own experience, with no way to step out of it,

24
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:39,180
so you start from how loud something feels in here and then fail to shrink the number enough for

25
00:01:39,260 --> 00:01:42,940
people standing over there. The room is not a panel of judges.

26
00:01:43,020 --> 00:01:46,800
It is a room full of people running the same calculation about themselves,

27
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:49,720
aimed at their own reflection rather than yours.

28
00:01:50,320 --> 00:01:53,020
The title of that paper is not about shirts, by the way.

29
00:01:53,320 --> 00:01:58,400
It is about actions and appearance. The shirt was simply the easiest thing to take off and

30
00:01:58,480 --> 00:02:02,270
count. Your face is the harder version of the same experiment.

31
00:02:02,520 --> 00:02:05,680
It runs every day, and it has no end time.

32
00:02:05,760 --> 00:02:08,220
There is a second reason it feels so certain.

33
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:10,640
You have almost never seen your own face.

34
00:02:10,720 --> 00:02:15,240
What you have seen, every day since you were tall enough to reach a sink, is the mirror version,

35
00:02:15,320 --> 00:02:20,380
flipped left to right, and that flipped version is the one your brain filed as you.

36
00:02:20,980 --> 00:02:24,140
In 1977, the researchers Theodore Mita,

37
00:02:24,480 --> 00:02:30,320
Marshall Dermer and Jeffrey Knight photographed people and then showed each of them two prints:

38
00:02:30,900 --> 00:02:35,100
the real photograph, and a mirror-reversed copy of it.

39
00:02:35,180 --> 00:02:38,849
People preferred the reversed one, the face they already knew.

40
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:43,120
Their close friends, handed the same two prints,

41
00:02:43,340 --> 00:02:46,340
preferred the real one almost every time.

42
00:02:46,940 --> 00:02:51,380
Nobody in that study was wrong. They were being asked about two different faces.

43
00:02:51,640 --> 00:02:54,800
You live with one of them. Everybody else lives with the other.

44
00:02:55,080 --> 00:02:57,440
So the photograph that ruins your evening,

45
00:02:57,540 --> 00:03:02,530
taken on somebody else's phone at an angle you did not choose, is not a discovery.

46
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:07,780
It is your actual face, seen by the one person alive who is not used to it.

47
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:12,520
Underneath both of those sits something far older than mirrors,

48
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:16,140
cameras, or the whole idea of a photograph.

49
00:03:16,380 --> 00:03:20,790
A fast, automatic scorer that rates a face the instant it arrives,

50
00:03:20,900 --> 00:03:23,620
before any deliberate thought catches up.

51
00:03:24,210 --> 00:03:30,520
The psychologists Judith Langlois and Lori Roggman found the cleanest evidence of what it is

52
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:36,380
tuned for. When they blended dozens of ordinary faces into a single composite image,

53
00:03:36,460 --> 00:03:41,780
people rated the blend as more attractive than nearly every individual face that went into it.

54
00:03:42,269 --> 00:03:45,880
The scorer is not hunting for something rare or exceptional.

55
00:03:45,960 --> 00:03:50,060
It is hunting for the middle. For symmetry, for regularity,

56
00:03:50,140 --> 00:03:53,040
for a face that reports no particular problem.

57
00:03:53,120 --> 00:03:58,480
And a judgment that fires that fast, before you can think, is not a matter of taste.

58
00:03:58,760 --> 00:04:02,600
Speed like that is reserved for things that used to be urgent.

59
00:04:02,680 --> 00:04:07,320
Here is what that scorer was actually for, and it is not what you have been using it for.

60
00:04:07,400 --> 00:04:10,480
It was built to point outward. At other people.

61
00:04:10,560 --> 00:04:12,980
For almost the entire span of human existence,

62
00:04:13,060 --> 00:04:16,400
it never once got a clear look at the face it belonged to.

63
00:04:17,160 --> 00:04:20,140
Think about what a person could genuinely see of themselves.

64
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:24,380
Still water on a windless day. A wet stone.

65
00:04:25,260 --> 00:04:28,800
A sliver of polished rock, if their group happened to own one.

66
00:04:29,409 --> 00:04:32,240
Their own hands, their own feet,

67
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:37,520
and nothing above the neck. For roughly two hundred thousand years,

68
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:43,211
the single most examined object in your life today was the one object a human being was

69
00:04:43,291 --> 00:04:49,000
structurally unable to examine. The verdict on your face lived somewhere you could not read it

70
00:04:49,080 --> 00:04:52,389
directly, in the faces of everyone else as they looked at you.

71
00:04:52,469 --> 00:04:57,000
The mirror did not fix that, because the mirror is barely here yet.

72
00:04:57,080 --> 00:05:00,430
A clear, sharp, honest mirror is an industrial product,

73
00:05:00,700 --> 00:05:04,720
cheap enough for an ordinary home only in the last couple of centuries.

74
00:05:04,800 --> 00:05:09,720
Before that it was a rich person's object, or a dim sheet of polished metal,

75
00:05:09,980 --> 00:05:13,980
or nothing at all. So the arithmetic is worth sitting with.

76
00:05:14,240 --> 00:05:17,060
Two hundred thousand years of a face you could not inspect,

77
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:19,940
then roughly two hundred years of one you can,

78
00:05:20,020 --> 00:05:25,090
then about fifteen years of carrying a lit camera that points at it on demand.

79
00:05:25,380 --> 00:05:28,550
Your scorer has not had time to notice any of that happened.

80
00:05:28,630 --> 00:05:31,960
And the people it was built to read were not strangers.

81
00:05:32,310 --> 00:05:35,520
A person lived and died inside a group of a few hundred at most,

82
00:05:35,600 --> 00:05:41,450
and by adulthood had seen every one of those faces thousands of times, in every light,

83
00:05:41,560 --> 00:05:46,200
at every age. Nobody scrolled past a face and never saw it again.

84
00:05:46,280 --> 00:05:52,320
Everyone you rated, you would hunt beside, marry into, or depend on when your own food ran out.

85
00:05:53,560 --> 00:05:58,920
That is the world the evolutionary anthropologist Sarah Blaffer Hrdy has spent a career

86
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:03,220
reconstructing, and inside it appearance was never decoration.

87
00:06:03,760 --> 00:06:08,080
It was a signal the whole group read at once: health, fertility,

88
00:06:08,200 --> 00:06:10,820
the alliances standing behind you.

89
00:06:11,580 --> 00:06:16,360
In Hrdy's account, a woman's standing among the other women ran partly on exactly this kind of

90
00:06:16,440 --> 00:06:20,560
comparison, because mates and allies and protection were finite,

91
00:06:20,640 --> 00:06:23,700
and everyone was quietly ranked against everyone else.

92
00:06:24,160 --> 00:06:27,720
None of this ran on flattery. In a small food-sharing group,

93
00:06:27,800 --> 00:06:32,740
standing decided who shared meat with you after a failed hunt, whose fire you were welcome at,

94
00:06:32,820 --> 00:06:39,200
whose children you were trusted to hold. Sliding down that ranking was not a bad mirror day.

95
00:06:39,280 --> 00:06:42,190
It could mean losing the people who fed you.

96
00:06:42,270 --> 00:06:45,380
The system is loud because it earned the right to be loud.

97
00:06:45,480 --> 00:06:48,770
And yet the standard it was scoring against was never fixed.

98
00:06:48,850 --> 00:06:53,840
The anthropologist Judith Anderson and her colleagues compared body ideals across dozens of

99
00:06:53,920 --> 00:06:59,480
societies and found a pattern that no theory of universal beauty predicts.

100
00:06:59,560 --> 00:07:04,440
Where food was scarce and unreliable, fuller bodies were the attractive ones.

101
00:07:05,480 --> 00:07:10,140
Where food was steady and dependable, the preference flipped toward slim.

102
00:07:10,840 --> 00:07:12,810
The reason is not mysterious.

103
00:07:12,890 --> 00:07:15,160
Where a body might have to survive a shortage,

104
00:07:15,440 --> 00:07:20,070
visible reserves were a genuine advantage worth choosing in a mate or an ally.

105
00:07:20,870 --> 00:07:26,180
Where shortages did not come, those same reserves stopped signalling anything useful,

106
00:07:26,440 --> 00:07:29,740
and something else took over as the marker of health.

107
00:07:30,410 --> 00:07:34,250
The standard your brain treats as bedrock has been quietly rewritten,

108
00:07:34,500 --> 00:07:39,660
society by society, according to what a group could afford to want.

109
00:07:39,740 --> 00:07:44,540
It was never issuing a verdict on you. It was reading the room you happened to be born into.

110
00:07:45,140 --> 00:07:47,180
Now put that system in your bathroom.

111
00:07:47,900 --> 00:07:52,820
Every morning, a stranger-rating scorer that was never given access to its own owner is handed a

112
00:07:52,900 --> 00:07:59,120
large, well-lit, permanently available, left-right reversed image of exactly that owner.

113
00:07:59,200 --> 00:08:03,680
So it does what it does. It rates. You pass a shop window and it rates again.

114
00:08:03,760 --> 00:08:08,020
You open the front camera and it rates a version it has never been trained on.

115
00:08:08,100 --> 00:08:11,460
You join a video call and the preview window shows the true,

116
00:08:11,540 --> 00:08:15,920
unreversed face while a lifetime of mirrors trained you on the opposite,

117
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:21,080
which is why you can feel perfectly fine walking down a street and wrong the second a laptop lid

118
00:08:21,160 --> 00:08:25,820
opens. Then, and this part has no ancestral equivalent whatsoever,

119
00:08:26,020 --> 00:08:31,399
you scroll. Your comparison group used to be a few hundred faces you had known since birth.

120
00:08:31,920 --> 00:08:33,950
Now it is thousands of faces a week,

121
00:08:34,039 --> 00:08:38,579
most of them chosen by a recommendation system precisely because they perform well,

122
00:08:38,659 --> 00:08:43,820
many of them smoothed, lit, retouched, or picked from three hundred attempts.

123
00:08:44,380 --> 00:08:49,300
That is not a population. It is a highlight reel with the ordinary middle removed.

124
00:08:49,480 --> 00:08:54,080
And your scorer, which cannot tell a neighbour from a stranger optimized by a machine,

125
00:08:54,240 --> 00:08:57,720
files every last one of them as local competition.

126
00:08:57,800 --> 00:09:02,360
So how much of your verdict is a measurement, and how much is just repetition?

127
00:09:02,440 --> 00:09:05,320
A tool built to rank a few hundred known faces,

128
00:09:05,400 --> 00:09:08,900
fed a reversed image of the one face it was never meant to see,

129
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,680
benchmarked against a population that does not exist,

130
00:09:11,760 --> 00:09:15,410
and run hundreds of times a week instead of a few times a season.

131
00:09:16,040 --> 00:09:18,980
Every pass feels like independent confirmation.

132
00:09:19,060 --> 00:09:21,600
It is the same broken measurement, taken again.

133
00:09:22,010 --> 00:09:25,020
None of this means the thing you see is imaginary.

134
00:09:25,100 --> 00:09:30,100
It may well be there. This is not a promise that you are secretly flawless and only need better

135
00:09:30,180 --> 00:09:33,480
lighting, and anyone selling you that is selling you something.

136
00:09:33,560 --> 00:09:38,820
What changes is not the feature. It is the weight on it, and where that weight came from.

137
00:09:38,900 --> 00:09:42,010
The spotlight effect says most of that weight was never in the room.

138
00:09:42,090 --> 00:09:46,360
The mirror mismatch says a good part of what you are rating is a face almost nobody else has

139
00:09:46,440 --> 00:09:51,400
ever seen. And the scorer underneath both was built to glance at a stranger once,

140
00:09:51,640 --> 00:09:55,360
not to run the same face ten thousand times across a single lifetime.

141
00:09:55,580 --> 00:09:58,360
So the shift tonight is not to like the feature.

142
00:09:58,520 --> 00:10:01,000
It is smaller than that, and more useful.

143
00:10:01,260 --> 00:10:04,060
It is to stop treating your own verdict as evidence.

144
00:10:04,140 --> 00:10:09,980
It will arrive anyway. In a mirror, in a lift door, in a photo you did not agree to.

145
00:10:10,060 --> 00:10:14,550
And when it does, the only thing worth asking is what it just measured you against.

146
00:10:14,980 --> 00:10:19,340
A few hundred people who already know you and will see you again tomorrow regardless.

147
00:10:19,420 --> 00:10:23,140
Or a scroll of strangers assembled by a machine to be looked at.

148
00:10:23,620 --> 00:10:25,940
Your reflection is not a neutral witness.

149
00:10:26,020 --> 00:10:31,200
It is the only witness in your life running on a reversed image, a private spotlight,

150
00:10:31,280 --> 00:10:36,580
and a comparison group that no human being before this century ever had to stand next to.

151
00:10:37,020 --> 00:10:39,820
You still know exactly what is wrong with your face.

152
00:10:39,900 --> 00:10:42,180
But that was never a fact about your face.

153
00:10:42,260 --> 00:10:48,140
It was a fact about the only pair of eyes ever assigned to watch it, at close range, in reverse,

154
00:10:48,220 --> 00:10:50,700
for a lifetime, with nowhere else to look.

155
00:10:50,920 --> 00:10:55,480
And the same tunnel that narrows your whole mind around a scarce number narrows just as hard

156
00:10:55,560 --> 00:10:59,940
around one feature on your face, until it is the only thing left in the frame.

157
00:11:00,040 --> 00:11:02,880
How many of today's verdicts came out of that same tunnel?

158
00:11:02,960 --> 00:11:07,520
That one already has a video of its own. The psychology of being poor.

159
00:11:07,720 --> 00:11:08,960
Watch that one next.
